# Vesopa Cloud: one master user, for signing a customer into their own webmail.
#
# The panel already knows who the customer is. Making them type a mailbox
# password to open an inbox they are already authenticated for is the friction
# the panel exists to remove — and it is a password people write down or reuse.
#
# Roundcube authenticates with SASL PLAIN proxy authorization: the mailbox is
# the authorization identity, this account is the authentication identity. So
# nothing — not the panel, not the database, not Roundcube — ever holds a
# customer's own mailbox password.
#
# result_success = continue is the line that makes this a MASTER user rather
# than simply a second account: once this passdb authenticates the master,
# Dovecot carries on to the normal passdb to find the mailbox being opened, and
# does not ask for its password.
#
# THIS FILE MUST BE INCLUDED BEFORE auth-passwdfile.conf.ext in 10-auth.conf.
# A master passdb is consulted for the authenticating identity and has to be
# reached first; listed after the normal one it is never tried and every SSO
# login fails with a plain "authentication failed".
passdb {
  driver = passwd-file
  args = /etc/dovecot/master-users
  master = yes
  result_success = continue
}
